# changes.psv -- what this collection changed or renamed in a program, shown
# on its card under Changed here.  name|note.  A key DIR/name (DIR under
# CMDS) picks one of two files that share a name.  Ports with ORIG/ and
# README.OSK get a pointer to that README automatically.
animal|Rebuilt from its source with one change: the frame is one column narrower than the screen, so column 80 is never written and the frames keep their shape in any window 80 or wider.
argproc_demo|Rebuilt from its source with 64K of memory; the build shipped before stopped with a stack overflow whatever it was given.
ascii|Built here from Roland McGrath's source.
atc|The `!' shell escape is left out; that key only redraws the screen.
bash|Two one-byte fixes: a start-up crash on systems with more than 16 MB of memory (BASH_VERSION now reads 1.12.12), and a pipe that waited for ever in `cat file | head -n 1', because every open path was handed to the command rather than paths 0 to 2.
bc|Its scanner now accepts CR as a line end as well as LF, so scripts written on this disk, piped input and its own maths library (bc -l) are no longer refused.
bks|Named bks, the posting's archive name, because this disk's `bs' is Battleships. Its ESC shell escape does nothing here, since OS-9 reads ESC as end of file.
blackjak|Rebuilt from its source so that it finds its SNOBOL4 syntax file in GAMES/SNOBOL from any directory; as built before, it looked in the current directory and died.
bmgtest|Opens a named file with OS-9's read mode: the Unix mode 0 grants no access here, so a search of a named file found nothing.
bmgtest2|Opens a named file with OS-9's read mode: the Unix mode 0 grants no access here, so a search of a named file found nothing.
boa|The binary is the porter's own, unchanged; its configuration file was changed to listen on port 8080, run as user 0, name a server and use this disk's directories.
browse|The `?' help key pages through less instead of more. And its help file names i, not k, for going up a line -- the OS-9 port moved that key and left the old letter in the help.
cfscores|The -a option and naming another player are gone, since OS-9 has no password database; it reports the player named by USER.
chemtab|The log of who ran the program is not kept.
cjpeg.070|Rebuilt from the jpeg-5a source on this disk, trap-free, named cjpeg.070 as before. The archive's gcc build answered `Premature end of input file' for every image and wrote nothing. This one also reads a netpbm file as written, CR between the header fields.
compress|The period binary was replaced by a rebuild of the same source with one fix: Microware's putchar read its argument twice on a pipe or terminal, so piped output was corrupt. Output to a file is byte-for-byte the same as before.
compress_4.0|Module name changed from compress to compress_4.0 to match its file, so loading it no longer answers for the compress in CMDS. Otherwise identical to the archive's.
corewar|Renamed from interp, its name in the posting.
cpp|One declaration guarded: cpp.h declared sprintf() returning char *, which this compiler's stdio.h contradicts, and nothing uses the value.
craps|The shell escape says it is unavailable, the dice-setting cheat is refused (it needs crypt(), which no library here has), and the score file is not locked.
creadoc|One byte of the binary changed, so it reads each file name from column 54 of the directory listing instead of 53. At 53 it took a leading space into the name and every open failed.
ctags.elvis|Elvis's ctags, renamed from ctags because the disk already has one; its module name was changed to match.
cwasm|Renamed from assem, its name in the posting.
cwdis|Renamed from disassem, its name in the posting.
devprc|Rebuilt from its source, replacing the archive module, which had a bad CRC and a damaged data descriptor and would not load. The copy on the EFFO disk is damaged the same way.
diff|GNU diff 1.4, built here from SRC/diff (the recipe in tools/rebuild/recipes.psv); the archive's GNU diff 1.1 is REBUILT/diff_1.1. The OS-9 port's date for the -c header is fixed: it had no line end, so the two header lines ran together and patch could not read them, and it showed the year as 126.
diff_1.1|Module name changed from diff to diff_1.1 to match its file, so loading it no longer answers for the diff in CMDS. Otherwise identical to the archive's.
dir|One instruction patched in the binary: it asked to write a line of 128 bytes, which the processor read as minus 128, so it could not list a directory. It now writes at most 127 characters a line and lists directories.
djpeg.070|Module name changed from djpeg to djpeg.070 to match its file, so loading it no longer answers for the djpeg in CMDS.
draw|Rebuilt from its source with one change: the canvas is 77 columns rather than 78 and the status line stops at column 79, so column 80 is never written and the frame keeps its shape in any window 80 or wider.
drawpoker|Named drawpoker because this disk's `poker' is a different game. The rules are paged with less, message pauses sleep instead of counting, the deal is seeded from the clock and it exits with status 0.
elvis_input|Elvis's input command. It was renamed input.elvis to avoid a clash, then elvis_input: elvis picks insert mode from the last letter of the name it is run by, and input.elvis ends in s, so it never opened in insert mode.
ephem881|Module name changed from ephem to ephem881 to match its file, so loading it no longer answers for the ephem in CMDS.
freeb|The volume name now prints without OS-9's high-bit terminator and the year in full, where the original printed the raw offset from 1900.
fstopgm|Stack size in its module header raised from 3K to 64K; it died of stack overflow before doing anything.
gnugo|Rebuilt from its source, replacing TOP's 1989 binary; no line of the C was changed.
graph|Module name changed from graph to Graph, the name the seven programs that use it ask for. Real OS-9 matches module names exactly, so as shipped none of them could have found it.
gzip020_csl|Module name changed from gzip to gzip020_csl to match its file, so loading it no longer answers for the gzip in CMDS. Otherwise identical to the archive's.
gzip68k_csl|Module name changed from gzip to gzip68k_csl to match its file, so loading it no longer answers for the gzip in CMDS. Otherwise identical to the archive's.
gzipcpu32k_csl|Module name changed from gzip to gzipcpu32k_csl to match its file, so loading it no longer answers for the gzip in CMDS. Otherwise identical to the archive's.
hackwish|Renamed from wish (its module was B_wish), since the WiSH shell here is also wish. Rebuilt with fixes so it forks hack by its full path and reads hack's prompts without blocking; before, it hung after the first game.
hang|The OS-9 port picked its word from only the first five lines of the dictionary, so every game was one of five words; rebuilt to count the file and choose from all of it.
hanoimod|Allocates room for its whole int array; the posted program allocated that many bytes and wrote past the end of the block on every move.
hipstopgm|Stack size in its module header raised from 3K to 64K; it died of stack overflow before doing anything.
imgtoppm|Stack size in its module header raised from 3K to 64K; it died of stack overflow before doing anything.
inews|Built with MNews's spool and command directories changed from the package's defaults, as its install notes ask, so it does not share UUCPbb's news spool.
infocom.tcap|Module name changed from infocom to infocom.tcap to match its file, so loading it no longer answers for the infocom in CMDS.
jumble2|Named jumble2, the posting's archive name, because this disk's `jumble' is a different program.
kermit2|Module name changed from kermit to kermit2 to match its file, so loading it no longer answers for the kermit in CMDS.
life|Built with 64K of memory instead of 16K; it recurses over the board and died of stack overflow after one generation. The board is 79 columns rather than 80, so it keeps its shape in a window wider than 80: curses relied on the terminal wrapping after the 80th column, and every row ran on into the next.
lnk.org|Module name changed from lnk to lnk.org to match its file, so loading it no longer answers for the lnk in CMDS.
lotto|At end of input it now exits; the posted program asked "Do you believe?" for ever.
ls|Built here from GNU fileutils 3.13 source, repaired for OS-9 so it shows real file modes, dates, owners and inode numbers.
m4|This is the archive's GNU m4 0.5 binary, unchanged. It took the name because the build from source that held it scrambled its output.
m4_0.5|Module name changed from m4 to m4_0.5 to match its file, so loading it no longer answers for the m4 in CMDS. Otherwise identical to the archive's.
macunpack|Renamed from macunpac, the archive's file name, to match the module name inside it.
make|Rebuilt with a utime() written for this collection: the original carried a copyright with no grant, and it wrote a zero over the file's link count on every touch.
monop|Two features that depend on a Unix process are disabled: the shell escape and saving or restoring a game.
moria|Rebuilt from TOP's source with a terminal-entry reader of its own, replacing TOP's binary, which could not find the terminal in this disk's termcap. The game code is unchanged. The map sits one column further left, the author's own BUGGY_CURSES layout, so column 80 is never written and the town keeps its shape in any window 80 or wider.
mscopy|With -t, a line copied to a DOS disk ends CR LF, as DOS expects; the port wrote LF CR. Two bytes patched in place.
msread|With -t, a line copied to a DOS disk ends CR LF, as DOS expects; the port wrote LF CR. Two bytes patched in place.
mstype|With -t, a line copied to a DOS disk ends CR LF, as DOS expects; the port wrote LF CR. Two bytes patched in place.
mswrite|With -t, a line copied to a DOS disk ends CR LF, as DOS expects; the port wrote LF CR. Two bytes patched in place.
mtools|With -t, a line copied to a DOS disk ends CR LF, as DOS expects; the port wrote LF CR. Two bytes patched in place.
mz|A timer that wrapped when the room was drawn within one clock tick is fixed (the game froze for ever), and the author's usage log is left out. The room is drawn with the cursor placed at the start of each row rather than left to the terminal's wrap after column 80, so it keeps its shape in any window 80 or wider.
newsetup|Built with its news directories changed to this disk's C News layout, and the site name changed from the porter's own to osk.UUCP.
newsrun|Built with its news-library directory names changed to this disk's C News layout; link() and rename() are versions written for this collection that copy the file, since RBF has no hard links.
nn|Built with MNews's spool and command directories changed from the package's defaults, and one #endif added to term.c, which the distributed file never closed.
nnadmin|Built with the directory it looks for nn's programs in changed from the package's default to where MNews lives on this disk.
nnaux|Built with the directory it looks for nn's programs in changed from the package's default to where MNews lives on this disk.
nncheck|Built with the directory it looks for nn's programs in changed from the package's default to where MNews lives on this disk.
nnmaster|Built with the directories nn looks in changed from the package's defaults to where MNews lives on this disk.
nobs|Rebuilt from its source with one change: it asks curses for single-key input at start-up. Without that, OS-9's curses returned at once from every key read, so it beeped without pause while waiting for a choice and never saw a key.
os9dsk|Rebuilt from its source rather than shipped as the author's binary, with coco_direct.h written for this collection in place of the release's header derived from Microware's direct.h.
patch|Every open() and creat() given OS-9 access modes: the Unix mode 0 grants nothing here, so patch fell back to Plan B and stopped with "Error reading tmp file", leaving the file untouched.
pbmtext|Stack size in its module header raised from 3K to 64K; it died of stack overflow before doing anything.
perp|The frame is 79 columns rather than 80, so it keeps its shape in any window 80 or wider; its bottom-right corner, which the original could not draw, is drawn, and the score, level and moves sit one column left inside it.
Pnews|Built with its news directories changed to this disk's C News layout, and the site name it signs articles with changed from the porter's own to osk.UUCP.
pnmtosir|Stack size in its module header raised from 3K to 64K; it died of stack overflow before doing anything.
poker|Rebuilt from its source so that it finds its SNOBOL4 syntax file in GAMES/SNOBOL from any directory; as built before, it looked in the current directory and died.
ppmnorm|Stack size in its module header raised from 3K to 64K; it died of stack overflow before doing anything.
ppmtoxpm|Stack size in its module header raised from 3K to 64K; it died of stack overflow before doing anything.
queens|Rebuilt from its IOCCC source (baruch.c) under its own name; the build that shipped first called itself B_baruch.
rdjpgcom.070|Module name changed from rdjpgcom to rdjpgcom.070 to match its file, so loading it no longer answers for the rdjpgcom in CMDS.
relaynews|Built with its news-library directory names changed to this disk's C News layout, and with a link() written for this collection that copies the file, since RBF has no hard links and the original raw-sector link() corrupted the lock.
rn|Built with its news directories changed to this disk's C News layout, and the site name it signs articles with changed from the porter's own to osk.UUCP.
MNEWS/rnews|Built with MNews's own spool and command directories, and it mails through UUCPbb's rmail where the package named another directory.
robots|Rebuilt from its source, which stopped it writing a stray control-K that scrolled the board, and it records scores under your USER name; the port's getlogin() answered "your name" for everyone. The version number sits one column further left, so column 80 is never written: the board keeps its shape, and its top-left border, in any window 80 or wider.
rot22|Named rot22, the archive's name, because this disk's `rot' is a different program. It also strips the falling-letter bit it printed as stray symbols on an 8-bit terminal.
rpoem|Rebuilt from its source so that it finds its SNOBOL4 syntax file in GAMES/SNOBOL from any directory; as built before, it looked in the current directory and died.
rsconvert|Memory size in its header raised from about 11K to 32K; its parser overflowed the stack converting a rayshade 3 scene.
rsdsk|Rebuilt from its source rather than shipped as the author's binary; nothing was changed.
rstory|Rebuilt from its source so that it finds its SNOBOL4 syntax file in GAMES/SNOBOL from any directory; as built before, it looked in the current directory and died.
sbatch|Built with MNews's spool and command directories changed from the package's defaults, as its install notes ask, so it does not share UUCPbb's news spool.
sed|This is the archive's GNU sed 1.06 binary, unchanged. It took the name because the build from source that held it answered every script with No more memory.
sed_1.06|Module name changed from sed to sed_1.06 to match its file, so loading it no longer answers for the sed in CMDS. Otherwise identical to the archive's.
NETPBM/sirtopnm|Rebuilt with one loop bound corrected: it skipped two bytes too few of the SIR header, so every colour plane came back shifted and a SIR file did not convert back to the picture it came from.
slice|Default output names are <input>.001 rather than <input>:001, and -m writes one mailbox file per day, since RBF names cannot carry a colon.
snake|Rebuilt from its source with two changes: echo is turned off while it plays (the port's raw() and cook() were empty, so each key typed was echoed onto the board), and stdout is no longer made unbuffered -- with it, the C library held back the ESC of each cursor move after a flush, so moves were printed on the board as text.
soelim|One token fixed in its usage message, which printed an empty name where the program's name belongs.
stone|Rebuilt from its source so that it finds its SNOBOL4 syntax file in GAMES/SNOBOL from any directory; as built before, it looked in the current directory and died.
tet|Replaced by a rebuild from its source with the keyboard compiled in (the build first shipped took no keys), then fixed so it really pauses between moves, turns echo off and keeps a score file.
top|Rebuilt from its source with its process-table walk bounded: it read past the end of its own table and died with a bus error just after printing its headings. The rebuild needs no cio.
torus|The field is one column narrower than the screen, so column 80 is never written and it keeps its shape in any window 80 or wider.
trek73|Saving and restoring a game are disabled: a save dumps the whole data segment, which OS-9 does not lay out that way.
trlf|Written for this collection: newsrun calls a trlf that exists in no archive, so this one turns LF into CR, in place or as a filter, and nothing more.
UnMacpack|Renamed from unmacpack, the archive's file name, to match the module name in its header.
unshar|Two bugs in the posted interpreter are fixed -- a temporary file closed wrongly and a clobber test that refused every file -- and a few shell forms other shar programs write were added.
unsit|Renamed from unsit_DJL, the archive's file name, to match the module name inside it.
utree|The Group command and the corner clock are left out, since OS-9 has no group database and no alarm signal; commands run through utree's SHELL variable rather than system().
uue|Three lines removed that defined fputc as putc: with them the translation table came out empty and uud refused every file.
vi|PVIC 1.0a. It shipped here as vi_nocio and took the name vi, with its module renamed to match, when the EFFO vi that held the name left the disk.
vi.elvis|Elvis's vi, renamed from vi because the disk already had a vi; its module name was changed to match.
vi_1.0|Renamed from VI, and its module from vi, so it no longer answers for the vi in CMDS. It is PVIC 1.0, the edition before the 1.0a that ships as vi.
vmod_trap|Module name changed from vmod_trap to VMod_trap, the name rxmod asks for. As found, rxmod could never have linked it on real OS-9.
wam.sbprolog|Built here from the SB-Prolog sources with their own OS-9 makefiles, replacing the prebuilt binary the archive carried; both gave identical output on the same queries.
wrjpgcom.070|Module name changed from wrjpgcom to wrjpgcom.070 to match its file, so loading it no longer answers for the wrjpgcom in CMDS.
xargs|The command is forked directly instead of through system() and the "exec " prefix is gone, so a redirection or pipe written in the command is not interpreted.
xmas|The two underscore rules are 79 columns rather than 80, so curses never relies on the terminal wrapping and the bottom rule stays on its own line -- it did not, even at 80 wide. Its sign-off is the author's own again; the 1990 OS-9 port had replaced it with a greeting of its own.
xpmtoppm|Stack size in its module header raised from 3K to 64K; it died of stack overflow before doing anything.
xvminitoppm|Stack size in its module header raised from 3K to 64K; it died of stack overflow before doing anything.
yahtzee2|The ! shell escape is removed.
yidslots|Named yidslots, without the hyphen the posting's yid-slots has, since no program name on this disk carries one.
zc|The `Y' key, which copied to a clipboard library that never existed, now rings the bell.
zoo_2.1|Module name changed from zoo to zoo_2.1 to match its file, so loading it no longer answers for the zoo in CMDS. Otherwise identical to the archive's.
bmg|Renamed from bm: here that name is K5JB's mailer, the program KA9Q's documentation calls bm.
NETWORK/net|Now K5JB's k37, built here from its own source, in place of the k35c binary that shipped without one. The year in its Received: lines is written as two digits; as K5JB left it, it read 126 after 1999.
NETWORK/bm|The year in its Date: header is written as two digits; as K5JB left it, it read 126 after 1999.
NETWORK/prexd|Built as prexd, not rexd, its author's name: OS-9/Net has a rexd of its own, and this one starts its server by name.
NETWORK/prexdc|Built as prexdc, not rexdc, its author's name, for the reason given on prexd's card.
GAMES/canfield|Fixed here: it opened its score file write-only, so no game was ever recorded, and it filed each player by the whole group.user word.  It now opens the file read-write and files players by user number.
GAMES/cfscores|Finds a player by user number, as canfield now files them; it used the whole group.user word.
argproc_demo|Rebuilt 2026-09-24 with the author's own vsprintf (the os9 build option): the collection's first build borrowed one that copies twelve words and cannot print %f, so its usage line lost its name and its pi, and a second -help could crash it.
discord|A bug in the program as posted, fixed here: both calls to chase() passed a word number counted from zero where chase() counts from one, so a draw of 0 returned an uninitialised pointer and crashed it -- one run in four on a four-word input.
perl|Two ownership fixes to this collection's port: -o, -O and the owner checks compared a 16-bit user number with getuid()'s whole group.user word, so they failed for everyone but the super-user; and the stat it linked had a file's group and user bytes swapped.
REBUILT/gtar|Two ownership fixes to this collection's port: a listing or extract took a file's group only from the group name, which OS-9 never has, so tvf showed group 0 and the super-user restored 1.7 as 31.7; and a rebuild's stat left the owner at 0, so the owner is now read from the file descriptor.
elm|One instruction changed so that it finds its user in the password file: it compared the file's user number with getuid(), which on OS-9 is the whole group.user word, so everyone but the super-user was told `You have no password entry!'.
filter|Two changes in place. It masked getuid() to the user number before a lookup that compares the whole group.user word, so for anyone outside group 0 it exited silently. And `save' now works: it wrote the message to a temp file named with its process id and read it back under a number never set, so every save ended in "can't open temp file".
logname|One instruction changed: it masked getuid() to the user number before a lookup that compares the whole group.user word, so it named only the super-user and told everyone else `no login name'.
frm|One byte changed: a header ended at a line starting with a line feed, where an OS-9 folder's blank line is a carriage return, so it counted no mail in a folder that had some.
newmail|One byte changed, the same as frm's: it looked for a line feed to end each header, where an OS-9 folder's blank line is a carriage return.
smail|One instruction changed so that a letter is signed with its sender's name: it compared the user number alone with getuid()'s whole group.user word, and signed everyone but the super-user `nobody'.
crontab|One constant changed: -u, which acts on another user's crontab, tested the high byte of the user number rather than the group, so any user numbered below 256 could use it. It now requires group 0.
mw|The module's owner in its header is 0.0 instead of 30.95, left from the machine it was linked on; the kernel lets a program take on another user only if the program's owner allows it, so everyone but the super-user got `Can't setuid'. No code changed.
collect|One byte changed: its scan loop tested a pointer not yet set, so it could skip the object and write an empty constructor table without a word. It now tests the start of the file, as later collect2 does.
gcc_collect|One byte changed: its scan loop tested a pointer not yet set, so it could skip the object and write an empty constructor table without a word. It now tests the start of the file, as later collect2 does.
gpp_collect|One byte changed: its scan loop tested a pointer not yet set, so it could skip the object and write an empty constructor table without a word. It now tests the start of the file, as later collect2 does.
lpq|Rebuilt from its source with one change: its job table compared the printer's name with a single character of it (queue->printer[n][0], where the name belonged), so it said `No Spooljobs' under a count of the jobs waiting. The [0] is gone.
top|Rebuilt from its source with one more change: it lists your own processes, busy or idle, and -a everyone's. It used to list only those that had used a tick since its last screen, so with nothing else running it showed its headings and little or nothing else.
pnmsmooth|Rebuilt from its source with two fixes: it filled pnmconvol's argument list before it knew the file names, so pnmconvol was handed nothing, and it wrote its kernel to a RAM disk at /r0; the names are filled in just before the fork, and the kernel goes to /dd/tmp.
shar|Rebuilt from its source with one change: its read-access test was inverted, so it answered `No read access' for every file it could read. It now wraps a readable file and refuses only one it cannot read.
xcrypt|Rebuilt from its source with two changes: it creates its output file (it asked open() to, which OS-9's open() cannot, so it stopped unless the file existed), and -e or -d names the direction -- the original took it from the name it was run by, so as `xcrypt' it could only decrypt.
tsmon2|Rebuilt from its source with one change: when it cannot open the line it prints the reason after its message; it used to leave that to Microware's shell.
uuencode|Changed in place, a few instructions: it takes the input file and the name to record, as its usage line says -- before, two arguments earned only the usage line. One argument still encodes that file under its own pathlist, and none prints the usage.
checkgame|Rebuilt from its source with two changes: it replays the last move of the list (it stopped one short), and a list that ends on white's move no longer makes it read past the end of the line.
game|Rebuilt from its source with the same two changes as checkgame -- it draws the last move, which it left out -- and each board line ends in one `]', where every board after the first carried two.
lpsched|One instruction changed: when a job queued with `lp -m' was done, it looked up whom to mail with a logical AND in place of a mask, so the mail went to user number 1 whoever queued the job. It now goes to the job's owner.
qp|Rebuilt from its source with one change: it hands the expanded command to $SHELL as the shell's one argument, the way ksh and Microware's shell take it. It passed the options `-ny -nl' of the EFFO zsh it was written for, which no shell here has, so it expanded nothing.
cuts|Rebuilt from its source, trap-free, with one change: the date in the header it writes has two year digits again -- from 2000 on it wrote three, and the decoder then lost the file's name and type. The archive build could not encode at all (it filled memory) and decoded only line ends.
crypto|Rebuilt from its source, trap-free. The archive build ended the whole session when used as a filter; this one encodes and writes its output.
unpacklib|Rebuilt from its source, trap-free. The archive build stopped at `Wrong SYNC long word' after the first module of every library; this one takes a library apart whole.
screen|Rebuilt from its source, trap-free, with one line fixed in scandir.c: a file counter was never set, and the archive build died with a bus error on the first file it picked.
lispmtopgm|Rebuilt from its source with two changes: its maxval is one less than 2 to the depth, so an 8-bit image is no longer refused, and it skips each row's padding, so an image whose width is not a multiple of 32 comes back row for row.
dm|Rebuilt from its source for an ANSI or vt100 terminal: the arrow keys move the bar as Ctrl-N and Ctrl-P do, the bar shows in reverse video where there is no colour, and it no longer needs cio.
editor|Rebuilt from its source; at the end of its input it now leaves as `!' does, where it redrew its menu for ever.
mkdict|Rebuilt from its source; a line longer than a word no longer overruns its buffer and stops it with a bus error.
bincheckr|Rebuilt from its source: it reads the book gnuchess 4.0 writes, where it read only an older layout, says so rather than dividing by zero when a book has no entries, and its odd and even counts are no longer swapped.
pacman|Rebuilt from its source: it takes its cursor motion from termcap, sets the terminal itself where it forked Microware's tmode, and no longer prints through an unbuffered stdout -- so its maze draws at an ANSI terminal.
hexedit|Rebuilt from its source against the disk's ncurses; the archive's build sent every cursor move to the same place and read TERMCAP only as the entry itself.
fuddle|Its TeleVideo screen codes are changed to their ANSI equivalents -- cursor addressing, clear, home, clear to end of line and the reverse video of its pieces (tools/patch_fuddle.py); there is no source.
cp|Eight bytes changed: run with too few names it printed its usage and then carried on into a bus error; it now stops after the usage, as it does for -?.
head|One byte changed: its close() handed back the path number where 0 means success, so every file it read was reported on standard error as an error and it exited 1.
todos|Its temporary is named todos.___.<n>, not todos.$$$.<n>, so the shell that renames it no longer reads `$$' as its process number; and the stray line feed it wrote after the last line is gone.
toos9|Its temporary is named toos9.___.<n>, not toos9.$$$.<n>, for the same reason as todos; and it no longer writes the $FF of end-of-file as the last byte.
ksh|Rebuilt from its source, trap-free, so it no longer needs cio. Two faults in `read' are fixed: a second `while read ...; done < file' in a script now reads its file, and a run of spaces between words counts as one separator. SRC/pdksh/README.OSK says what else the rebuild took.
